it's been nearly 50 years since Bruce  Lee died suddenly in the prime of his  life but his legend lives on her ways  that even he could never have imagined  here's a look at the highlights and  lowlights of his long and buried career  from the movies that made him an icon to  the ones we'd rather just forget Game of  Death was a movie famously built around  the footage Bruce Lee left behind from  his unfinished final film most of which  consisted of fight scenes that took  place on various levels of a pagoda  after Lee's death Enter the Dragon  director Robert Klaus took that footage  and integrated it into a new story about  a martial arts movie star being hunted  by a crime syndicate  Game of Death is also notorious for  integrating footage of Lee from his  other movies often to jarring effect and  at one point even uses footage of Lee's  actual body in his casket if Game of  Death had simply been made as a 70s  martial-arts flick pull-up subterfuge  and slick fight scenes it wouldn't be a  bad movie at all as it is the footage  that doesn't try to trick you into  thinking you're watching Bruce Lee a  solid the opening credits are killer and  John Barry's score is probably the best  thing about it watching it in context as  a film cobbled together from Lee's  unfinished work however makes it a  trying viewing experience and a sad  reminder of what might have been the  game of death was supposed to star Bruce  Lee as a retired martial artist who's  coerced into retrieving something  valuable from the top of a mysterious  pagoda with each level guarded by a  martial artist with a different fighting  style as you know however the true  version of the movie never came to  fruition thanks to Lee's untimely  passing what footage did exist was later  released as part of the documentary  Bruce Lee a warrior's journey the game  of deaths climax featured Lee and his  now iconic yellow tracksuit taking on  these martial artists in a series of  precisely choreographed battles  culminating in a fight scene with Kareem  abdul-jabbar's character at the pagoda  summit watching what exists of The Game  of Death is a bittersweet experience  because while we'll never get to see the  full film seeing what Lee was able to do  with this level of creative control and  precision is truly astonishing  nonetheless Bruce Lee's first major  introduction to American audiences was  as Kato in the Green Hornet the  superhero action-adventure series in  which he stole the show is sidekick to  the titular masked hero the show was  successful in America but overseas it  made Lee and even  bigger star to the point that in Hong  Kong it became known as the Kato show  lease international popularity is Catoe  combined with the mythology that sprung  up around named after his untimely death  in 1973 led Fox to consider repurposing  the old Green Hornet episodes to  capitalize on a Spain so in 1974 they  cut together several episodes from the  1966-67 series and released them as a  feature film it did well in some markets  but as you might expect from a heavily  episodic series like the Green Hornet  it doesn't actually work all that well  as a movie with a narrative through line  the way of the Dragon is the first film  in which Bruce Lee officially stepped  into the role of both director and  screenwriter giving him more creative  control than he'd had on any of his  previous works the story is relatively  simple a martial artist heads to a  foreign land to become an enforcer in a  fight to save innocent people from a  tyrannical crime boss however more than  any of his previous films this one is  truly infused with Lee's personality's  sense of humor and sense of pacing  because of that way of the Dragon is  funnier than Lee's previous Hong Kong  films as he plays the fish-out-of-water  with a huge amount of fun it's also got  a more ambitious sense of action as  exemplified by the scenes in which he  single-handedly takes on a gang of thugs  inside the restaurant at the center of  the conflict most famously though this  is the film in which Bruce Lee fights  Chuck Norris who made his big-screen  debut with Way of the Dragon  that bite which is set inside Rome's  Coliseum still holds up is one of the  coolest things to have ever happened in  martial arts cinema  [Music]  Bruce Lee's first major leading role in  a martial arts film was the big boss the  film he made after returning to Hong  Kong following a short and frustrating  an attempt to break Hollywood Lee plays  Cheng Chao an a young man who moves from  China to Thailand to live with family  members who are unknowingly connected to  a drug smuggling ring run out of an ice  factory things start to get rough almost  immediately but Chang can't join the  fight because of a promise he made to  his mother to never get involved in  violence let's be clear the big boss is  actually a pretty good movie it just  happens to be the worst of the major  martial arts films that Lee had  completed at the time of his death there  are some terrific fight scenes here and  Lee's on-screen swagger is palpable when  viewed alongside his later work though  you can easily see just how much further  he could really push himself sadly it's  impossible to separate the release of  Enter the Dragon from the death of its  star Bruce Lee died just weeks before  this film was released in America and  while it was supposed to make him a  global star by bringing his Hollywood  kung fu stylings to a bigger audience in  death it made him an icon Enter the  Dragon remains one of the most  culturally significant films of the  1970s a watershed moment that informed  and influenced countless martial arts  films that came after as well as action  cinema in general even when you try to  see the film outside the context of  Lee's death you'll still experience a  masterpiece Enter the Dragon is the work  of a young star who poured everything  he'd learned from his past hits into  this one perfection seeking mega hit it  remains a riveting film from Lee's  emotional content speech near the  beginning all the way to that legendary  mirrored room fight scene at the end  what was that an exhibition we emotional  content check out one of our newest  videos right here plus even more looper  videos about your favorite movies are  coming soon subscribe to our YouTube  channel and hit the bell so you don't  miss a single one  [Music]
 

